Let's test modifying configuration without touching the underlying template YAML.

1.Upgrade via Command-Line Flag (--set):

Method 1
Scale your deployment to 3 replicas on the fly without editing files:

helm upgrade dev-web ./web-app --set replicaCount=3

Verification: Run kubectl get pods -l app.kubernetes.io/instance=dev-web and see the Deployment scale to 3 running pods!

2.Create an Environment-Specific Values File

Method 2

In real production CI/CD pipelines, you rarely use --set. Instead, you maintain dedicated files like values-staging.yaml and values-prod.yaml.

Create a custom values file:

cat <<EOF > values-prod.yaml
replicaCount: 2
service:
  type: NodePort
  port: 8080
EOF

Apply it over your release:

helm upgrade dev-web ./web-app -f values-prod.yaml

Verification: Run kubectl get svc -l app.kubernetes.io/instance=dev-web to confirm the Service 
changed its type to NodePort and port to 8080.

3.Inspect Revision History and Rollback:

Release tracking

Helm records every upgrade as an atomic revision in the cluster

helm history dev-web

Now roll back to Revision 1:

helm rollback dev-web 1

Verification: Run helm history dev-web and kubectl get pods -l app.kubernetes.io/instance=dev-web. 
The replica count immediately drops back to 1!

Try running the --set replicaCount=3 upgrade and helm history dev-web. What does your revision
 history look like?


REVISION        UPDATED                         STATUS          CHART           APP VERSION     DESCRIPTION     
1               Fri Oct  9 05:44:42 2026        superseded      web-app-0.1.0   1.16.0          Install complete
2               Fri Oct  9 06:47:15 2026        superseded      web-app-0.1.0   1.16.0          Upgrade complete
3               Fri Oct  9 06:48:45 2026        superseded      web-app-0.1.0   1.16.0          Upgrade complete
4               Fri Oct  9 06:51:56 2026        superseded      web-app-0.1.0   1.16.0          Rollback to 1   
5               Fri Oct  9 06:55:24 2026        deployed        web-app-0.1.0   1.16.0          Upgrade complete

That helm history output illustrates Helm's release tracking model.

Notice key operational details from your table:

Append-Only History: Helm never edits history in-place. When you ran helm rollback dev-web 1 at Revision 4, Helm did not delete revisions 2 and 3; it synthesized Revision 1's exact manifest state and deployed it as a new forward-moving event (Rollback to 1).

Atomic State: Only Revision 5 has the status deployed. Revisions 1 through 4 are marked superseded.

Where this is stored: Helm 3 has no server component (no Tiller). Every single revision is stored directly inside your cluster a

kubectl get secrets -l owner=helm,name=dev-web

